<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>
        A projection program is a sequence of screens shown on a kiosk's projector or display, such as a welcome
        message, a video, the scoreboard or a dashboard. Build them on
        <a href="/manage/projectionprograms">Projection</a>.
    </FluentText>

    <HelpSection Title="Building a program">
        <ol>
            <li>Select <strong>New</strong>, and give the program a <strong>Program Name</strong> and optional description.</li>
            <li>Select the program in the list on the left.</li>
            <li>Drag steps from the toolbox on the right into the flow in the middle, after <strong>START</strong>, or
                select a step's <strong>Add</strong> button (the plus) in the toolbox to add it at the end without dragging.
                The built-in steps are <strong>Show Text</strong>, <strong>Play a Video</strong>, <strong>Dashboard</strong>,
                <strong>Live Capture</strong> (a camera or capture device), <strong>Delay</strong>, which keeps the
                previous screen up, and <strong>Wait for Game</strong>, which keeps it up until a laser game event.</li>
            <li>Fill in each step's settings, such as the text, the video file, or the dashboard. For a video,
                select <strong>Browse</strong>: only videos are shown, and you can open a folder, search, use
                <strong>Recent</strong>, or upload a new one.</li>
            <li>Right-click a step for more options, such as deleting it, then save.</li>
        </ol>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Waiting for a laser game">
        <FluentText>
            A <strong>Wait for Game</strong> step holds the program until something happens in a laser game, instead of
            moving on after a set time. It shows nothing new, so the step before it stays on screen. That lets one
            program run the whole cycle, for example:
        </FluentText>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Play a Video</strong>: your attract loop.</li>
            <li><strong>Wait for Game</strong>, <strong>Get ready</strong>: the video stays up until players get ready.</li>
            <li><strong>Dashboard</strong>: the live scoreboard.</li>
            <li><strong>Wait for Game</strong>, <strong>Game ends</strong>: the scoreboard stays up until the game finishes.</li>
            <li><strong>Show Text</strong> with Last game winner: the results, then back to the start.</li>
        </ol>
        <FluentText>Its settings:</FluentText>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Wait until</strong>:
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Get ready</strong>: the kiosk shows Get ready, or a game starts (so a missed Get ready doesn't
                        hold a whole game). Moves on straight away if it's already getting ready or playing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Game starts</strong>: a game is in progress. Moves on straight away if one already is.</li>
                    <li><strong>Game ends</strong>: the game that's running finishes. If players are still getting ready, it
                        waits for that game to finish. Moves on straight away if no game is running or about to start,
                        as there's nothing to wait for.</li>
                    <li><strong>Time left drops below</strong>: there are <strong>Seconds Left</strong> or fewer left in the
                        game. It also moves on if the game stops early.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Laser Kiosk</strong>: the kiosk that runs the laser game. It can be a different kiosk from the one
                showing the program, and you must choose one.</li>
            <li><strong>Give Up After (minutes)</strong>: moves on anyway after this long, for example when the laser kiosk
                is switched off. Leave it blank or 0 to wait for as long as it takes. Time while the program is paused
                doesn't count.</li>
        </ul>
        <HelpNote>
            A kiosk that's lost its connection to Lanyard plays its saved copy of the program, and leaves out Wait for Game
            steps, along with dashboards.
        </HelpNote>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Live venue data in text">
        <FluentText>
            Text steps can show live data instead of fixed words. Change <strong>Text source</strong> from
            <strong>Fixed text</strong> to Last game winner, Today's top score or Opening hours today. The screen
            keeps it up to date while it's showing. Set <strong>Fallback text</strong> for when there's no data yet or
            the kiosk is offline.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Party details on screens">
        <FluentText>
            With Parties switched on, a text step can also show the party that's on now:
        </FluentText>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Party welcome</strong>: "Happy 7th birthday, Ava!" (or "Happy birthday, Ava!" with no age).</li>
            <li><strong>Birthday child's first name</strong>: just "Ava", for artwork with its own wording.</li>
            <li><strong>Party: what's next</strong>: the party's next laser tag or food time, such as "Laser tag at 14:30".</li>
        </ul>
        <FluentText>Which party a screen shows:</FluentText>
        <ul>
            <li>A party shows from 15 minutes before it starts until it ends.</li>
            <li>If the kiosk has a <strong>Party room</strong> (in the kiosk's Settings, General tab), it shows the party in
                that room. Otherwise, or when nothing is on in that room, it shows a party at the kiosk's location.</li>
            <li>When several parties are on, it shows the one that started most recently; before any has started, the next to
                start.</li>
            <li>Cancelled parties, provisional Just Step bookings and private hires are never shown.</li>
        </ul>
        <HelpNote>
            Names only appear when an Admin turns on <strong>Show the birthday child's first name on screens</strong> in
            <a href="/manage/parties/settings">Party settings</a>. Until then the welcome and name show their fallback text.
            Only the first name is ever shown, it's never saved on the kiosk, and an offline kiosk shows the fallback.
        </HelpNote>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Starting a program on a kiosk">
        <FluentText>Programs are started by:</FluentText>
        <ul>
            <li>an <a href="/help/automation">automation rule</a>, for example when a game ends;</li>
            <li>a dashboard <strong>Button</strong> widget set to Trigger Projection Program.</li>
        </ul>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
